	  \hat M^{(\mathrm{Line},X),\ell_X}_{f_X}.
	\]
	The answers are polynomial representatives of degree at most $md$.
	Positivity of each sandwich and completeness of the expanded projective
	line measurements make this a POVM. This auxiliary definition asserts
	no consistency with a joint point family.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Formalization support: the exact X marginal]
	\label{lem:combined-line-measurement-x-marginal}
	\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.ProjectiveSetting.combinedLineMeasurement_sum_Z}
	\leanok
	\uses{def:combined-line-measurement}
	For either player side and every pair of lines, the constructed POVM
	satisfies
	\[
	 \sum_{f_Z}T^{\ell_X,\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}
	 =\hat M^{(\mathrm{Line},X),\ell_X}_{f_X}.
	\]
	This auxiliary identity is used in the proof of Claim~17-2.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
	\leanok
	\uses{def:combined-line-measurement}
	Sum the middle Z effect to the identity and use projectivity of the
	outer X effect.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Formalization support: the concrete X-overlap deficit]
	\label{lem:concrete-x-overlap-deficit}
	\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.exists_concreteXPointOverlap_deficit_le}
	\leanok
	\uses{def:combined-line-measurement, lem:qld-sublines-direct,
		def:expanded-point-measurement, def:expanded-state}
	There is a universal $K>0$ such that, for every projective setting and
	auxiliary subline law above, the concrete measurement satisfies
	\[
	 1-\E_{(\ell,\ell_X,\ell_Z)\sim D_{\rm dir}}\E_{t\in\F_q}
	 \sum_{f_X,f_Z}\operatorname{Re}
	 \bra{\hat\psi}
	 (T^{\ell_X,\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z})_{\mathsf{A}\mathsf{A}'}
	 (\hat M^{(\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{f_X(x)})_{\mathsf{B}\mathsf{A}''}
	 \ket{\hat\psi}
	 \le K m^2\delta_{\mathrm{Line}}(\eps),
	\]
	where $\ell=(v,w)$, $x=(v+tw)_X$, and the
	completed point effect is zero at an undefined evaluation. This is an
	auxiliary estimate for Claim~17-2 on the directly indexed law.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
	\leanok
	\uses{lem:combined-line-measurement-x-marginal,
		lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds, lem:qld-comm-line-cons}
	The exact X marginal reduces the overlap to expanded X-line--point
	consistency. Apply its bound to each restricted component of the
	separate X marginal mixture and average the resulting deficits.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Auxiliary real-part estimate for the constructed line measurement]
	\label{lem:claim-17-2-direct-real}
	\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.subline_remove_X_factor}
	\leanok
	\uses{def:combined-line-measurement, lem:qld-sublines-direct,
		def:expanded-point-measurement}
	There is a universal $C>0$ such that, for every projective setting and
	auxiliary subline law above, setting
	$T^{\ell_X,\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}
	 =\hat M^{(\mathrm{Line},X),\ell_X}_{f_X}
	  \hat M^{(\mathrm{Line},Z),\ell_Z}_{f_Z}
	  \hat M^{(\mathrm{Line},X),\ell_X}_{f_X}$ gives
	\[
	 |\operatorname{Re}A_{ZX}(T)-\operatorname{Re}A_Z(T)|
	 \le C m\sqrt{\delta_{\mathrm{Line}}(\eps)}.
	\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
	\leanok
	\uses{def:combined-line-measurement,
		lem:combined-line-measurement-x-marginal, lem:concrete-x-overlap-deficit}
	Completeness of the Z-line measurement and projectivity of the X-line
	measurement give $\sum_{f_Z}T_{f_X,f_Z}=\hat M^X_{f_X}$ exactly.
	The separate X marginal mixture transfers expanded line-point
	consistency to a deficit at most $K m^2\delta_{\mathrm{Line}}(\eps)$.
	Projective Cauchy--Schwarz bounds the real-part difference by its
	square root, so $C=\sqrt K$ suffices. No paired-line consistency
	hypothesis is used in this calculation.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Auxiliary real-part estimate for the Z correlation]
	\label{lem:claim-17-3-direct-real}
	\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.subline_Z_term_near_one}
	\leanok
	\uses{lem:qld-4-10-same-placement, lem:overlap-gap-distance,
		lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds}
	There is a universal $C>0$ such that, under the stated point and line
	measurement hypotheses,
	\[
	 |\operatorname{Re}A_Z(T)-1|\le
	 C\sqrt m\,(\delta_P^{1/4}+\delta_Q^{1/4}+\eps^{1/4}).
	\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
	\leanok
	The separate Z marginal mixture bounds the deficit by
	$2\sqrt{4m^2\delta_P}+2\sqrt{4\delta_Q}$. Since the real overlap is
	in $[0,1]$, its squared deficit is no greater than its deficit.
	Taking a square root and using $m\ge1$ gives the bound with $C=2$.
\end{proof}

